# Collaboration & Clarification

When working on this project with the user:

## Clarify before building
- When the user describes a UI change or behavior, **confirm the main constraint** in one sentence before writing a lot of code (e.g. “So the inner top of each leg sits on the bottom point of the triangle?”).
- If the user provides a reference (sketch, mockup, screenshot, “like this”), treat that as the **source of truth** and describe how the implementation will match it.

## When the user is frustrated
- **Pause and ask** instead of guessing again.
- Use 1–2 short questions to lock in what’s wrong and what “right” looks like, e.g.:
  - “Which part is wrong: [concrete options]? Should it be higher, lower, or more in/out?”
  - “Can you point to the one rule that defines it (e.g. ‘inner top = bottom of triangle’)?”

## Plan before big changes
- For multi-step or layout work, **outline 2–3 steps** and get a quick “yes” or “like this” before changing a lot of code.
- If a task requires a lot of thinking (multi-step design, edge cases, or non-trivial logic), **switch to planning**: create or use a plan in `.cursor/plans/` and get alignment before making large code changes.
- Prefer small, reversible steps so we can correct course without big reverts.

## Reference over abstraction
- Prefer the user’s drawing or step-by-step geometry over deriving everything from abstract terms (“apex”, “slope”, “align”).
- If the user says “like in the image” or “as in the drawing”, treat that as the spec and match it; don’t substitute a different interpretation.

## Break Work Down into Smaller Testable Segments
- When tackling a planning project, remember to split the task into smaller chunks that we can implment and test before moving on to the next phase. Where possible.